f 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050322 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050322 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050322 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050322 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050322 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050322 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050322 #edb/ref 1031 "Calculating the Inverse of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050322 #edb/ref 1033 627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050322 #edb/ref 1034 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050322 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3fe45327-8fca-5b0c-bacd-05a7806eaeef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158876 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158876 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158876 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158876 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158876 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158876 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158876 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158876 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158876 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6a88399b-1bc1-50e9-b55f-1a6bb18a1b16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159638 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159638 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159638 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159638 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "02e0b4df-b03a-5331-8fad-a3680e2a7c52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161852 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161852 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y = X^4,$ where $X\\in (0,2)$\n\nII. $Y = \\cos X,$ where $X\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$\n\nIII. $Y = e^{-X},$ where $X\\in (0, \\infty)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161852 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14830c55-32bd-5eb9-adb6-b91675061a21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161852 #edb/ref 1074 "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y = u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x) = x^4$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,2).$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. For example, $u(x) = \\cos x$ is decreasing on $x \\in \\left(0,\\pi \\right)$ and increasing on $x \\in \\left(\\pi,2\\pi\\right).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x) =e^{-x}$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161853 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9101" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161853 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n![](images/2f/2f79ae967525e98c74b1fd9e1b7ea662be2d98b9b735895183af5a605f01ae26.svg)\n\nIf ![](images/15/15e9b40585f16c4115be3fecd5fa7787d56d49bdb5a948489e5898e5d2489bf3.svg) use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of ![](images/02/029615e92d8b6d5c444a4daf74a9a8d63048e8d4be5f97c8207fe7af2e189e18.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2fd76ea7-ab08-5afa-9762-158a85999321" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161853 #edb/ref 1074 "If ![](images/cf/cf4f5af6e36a7bb266b7ca27441291b77cf97f95ec83a87b3b8c1985c1d46795.svg) is a continuous random variable with probability density function ![](images/e5/e548073e412ef1d2ce28be65200ecee6d2de2d313f96926efc3f502c882a8b63.svg) and ![](images/db/db2d2f1b1e3647c4e4853b6cc42f6a39265528b3173bb1bfe63a66104d929977.svg) where the function ![](images/d0/d0cd8243c8a07ff42c8c7d04f1bb61abe699f8928d7d64ae9ee21ed045c5b871.svg) is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) is given by\n![](images/42/424a91756b7c3100fb1bb5922a8fefe7015fc768942814d300b89f76e39b0c85.svg)\n\nThe transformation \n![](images/8b/8b9847f92648081a5dc00186b1d87043357062ec996de29d445fec7ced30d4c6.svg)\nis strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ![](images/8d/8d18d6d491054e2e558053979a6359a2c40bd8741daf6f41154d2226fbd4026f.svg). Since\n\n![](images/da/dac66813f69cda0a79fb3368cebad35b827a7f7f3e4ec6067097c898ffc8af19.svg)\n\nwe solve for ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg):\n\n![](images/82/82ac91b2ad39ba66f694f75cd2c1632e671d8ef78fa4ab2730ff156980d1131c.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/25/25cba9a16acbf8dc5641c1b03c327025af1fd6c501d1fbba7912777e3b207a11.svg)\n\nThen, we find ![](images/4e/4ef82925f27bb344424f04e50f8e06e2043635483000894b7afff7cb4cc53552.svg)\n![](images/d7/d778066651368c14be06529c487d0af16be1384f36b34b6bbf55d86ecc7ebe6b.svg)\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ![](images/49/4958bae6d475dfd7b69ba62c4206ff577a7aca89978d096a7255df5de68dc7d4.svg) we have\n\n![](images/33/33abe4f49bf243c393397ab38ea454192378fd9c16868e6c9e1dd699625c8ebd.svg)\n\nFinally, since ![](images/e5/e548073e412ef1d2ce28be65200ecee6d2de2d313f96926efc3f502c882a8b63.svg) is nonzero on ![](images/60/60ded9db077de95e8e12a1697b6e1cfcb668709e61711a2de9235f93b1e43e47.svg) the bounds are ![](images/0d/0de2ecb189258e452bbbd4c453016da8d6a7ec1857d973f1591bc9ca5acc02f3.svg) and ![](images/24/24db93fbc4799e38a2ce26b39cf98b38c6ab418b087a71fe3aab35a0d5f525a6.svg) we have\n![](images/96/96c9789a1c16d9f1c042fc312c26cd96cfb1942c88bc282092c8e92baa60d981.svg)\n\nTherefore, the probability density function of ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) is\n\n![](images/07/07515c45f97b814ea1a7a01ca8b9ecf43a7f2e54c0a203d86653ccda3110f309.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161854 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9102" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161854 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n![](images/b4/b48edaa6312ced870bff2220f801f4e2da867a6665db2126311f2cfe28a4d558.svg)\n\nIf $Y = \\sqrt{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd8ceb2f-55e5-588f-ae79-015f58007fba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161854 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X \\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f_X$ and $Y = u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n![](images/ca/cacd5fa85f055d885fabb6ffa2cf62788d88482eeaef327182d052f6767b3a3b.svg)\n\nHere, we have $Y = \\sqrt{X}.$ The transformation $u(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find $u^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $(u^{-1})'(y){:}$\n\n$$\n(u^{-1})'(y) = 2y\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to the change-of-variable method, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({y}^{2})\\cdot |2y| \\\\  & =3({y}^{2})^{2}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =3{y}^{4}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =6{y}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $f_X$ is nonzero on $0 < x < 1,$ the bounds are $a = 0$ and $b = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0) = 0, \\quad u(1) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n![](images/e2/e20ebb32777cc624f0c436e59cb0be55958ab8369b345a7da890a3504b42fe34.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162052 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162052 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162052 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162052 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162052 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162052 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162052 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162052 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162052 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Transformations Where the Change-of-Variables Method Is Applicable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162052 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162052 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "233ee0d9-ce64-5d75-ac81-351ec0ac8f4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162053 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162053 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162053 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162053 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162053 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162053 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162053 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162053 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162053 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162053 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162053 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162053 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "26854b8e-68bc-5e69-8fcd-fa46b72d05c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162054 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162054 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162054 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162054 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162054 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162054 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162054 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162054 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162054 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162054 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162054 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162054 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "90802916-605f-5a66-b05b-908388c01ee9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162192 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162192 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162192 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162192 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162192 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162192 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162192 #edb/ref 1045 "The Change-of-Variables Method for Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162192 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162192 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162192 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162192 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "33fa95a4-1ec1-5d26-8a37-443af950cbe7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162279 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186158876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162279 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109183" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162279 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162279 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{x}, & 1<x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162279 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1fc46307-e5a1-551b-b8cf-8fa5407926f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162279 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{-1}(y)) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)={e}^{y}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({e}^{y})\\cdot |{e}^{y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{{e}^{y}}\\cdot {e}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{y/2}\\cdot {e}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{3y/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $1<x<4,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=4,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=0,\\,u(4)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{3y/2}, & 0<y<\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162406 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162406 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162406 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162406 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}, & 1<x<e \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{1}{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162406 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f7b4c3d-9f53-5a0a-9f65-57f007102f92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162406 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\frac{1}{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y})\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{y})}{(\\frac{1}{y})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $1<x<e,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=e,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=1,\\,u(e)={e}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{y}, & {e}^{-1}<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162954 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162954 #edb/ref 1061 33486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162954 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4d93e146-01aa-543e-9cbb-e868641f90cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162954 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162966 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162966 #edb/ref 1061 33924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162966 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b79655f1-bb54-5c12-b761-06cd72a1c5ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162966 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162967 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162967 #edb/ref 1061 33925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162967 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5dfe3a6-d6eb-5bee-97c5-ade0ebcb344a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162967 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162968 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162968 #edb/ref 1061 33926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162968 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "18b53d37-ee53-59da-9080-3399c63c1c76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162968 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162969 #edb/ref 1061 33927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162969 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "95cd8c7f-a4dd-5ba6-ad88-eab8ac6726e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162969 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163264 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable whose probability density function is known. We know that if $Y = u(X),$ then we can apply the distribution function method to compute the PDF of $Y.$\n\nIt's possible to generalize the distribution function method when $u(X)$ is strictly monotonic. This generalization is known as the **change-of-variables method**, and is stated below:\n\n*If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f_X$ and $Y = u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by*\n![](images/0c/0cef4bed9fd4d4e598259a8082f29e9c20d65537c8eb30591c153870cd202724.svg)\n\nProof of this result will be provided at the end of the lesson. \n\nThe advantage of the change-of-variables method is that it's often easier and faster to implement than the distribution function method. The disadvantage is that it only works when $u$ is monotonic, whereas the distribution function method can be extended to deal with non-monotonic functions. \n\nFor example, suppose that $X$ has the probability density function\n![](images/a3/a34a43fe641955493bc4e807d3dbd62bf47136a6e70278f9f7251a391da9bf80.svg)\n\nLet the random variable $Y = 3X.$ Then $u(x) = 3x,$ which is strictly monotonically increasing. Therefore, the change-of-variables method can be used to compute the PDF of $Y.$\n\nFirst, we find $u^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =3y \\\\ y & =3{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{1}{3}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $(u^{-1})'(y){:}$\n\n$$\nu^{-1}(y) = \\dfrac{1}{3}y \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad (u^{-1})'(y) = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero $f_Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{1}{3}y)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}y)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $f_X$ is nonzero on $0 < x < 2,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=2,$ and we have\n\n$$\nu(0) = 0, \\qquad u(2) = 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n![](images/4b/4bbe9255e26b47ee3d5afcb826dcc4c0da24e4786eb0ea74b964c2237829f0ae.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163264 #edb/ref 1065 5356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163264 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "86b37435-f432-5855-9152-51435d0e102c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163264 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163272 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's restate the change-of-variables method:\n\n*If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f_X$ and $Y = u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by*\n![](images/3d/3dfea9417dbbdf6f9f4e828a29bef40d59b4d467fb08fee100d326a6bfd8e00b.svg)\n\nNow, let's prove this result. \n\nThe first part is simple. Because $X$ is between $a$ and $b,$ and $u$ is monotonic, $Y=u(X)$ must be between $u(a)$ and $u(b).$ So $f_Y(y) = 0$ when $y$ is not between $u(a)$ and $u(b).$\n\nTo carry out the rest of the proof, we will need to apply the distribution function method in two cases: \n\n- **Case 1**: $u$ is monotonically increasing\n\n- **Case 2**: $u$ is monotonically decreasing\n\nWe'll consider the case where $u$ is monotonically increasing only. The proof when $u$ is monotonically decreasing is similar.\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative distribution of $Y.$ Assuming $y$ is between $u(a)$ and $u(b),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(u(X)\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le {u}^{-1}(y)) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{{u}^{-1}(y)}{f}_{X}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(t){|}_{a}^{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\  & ={F}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))-{F}_{X}(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we differentiate to get the probability density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[{F}_{Y}(y)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[{F}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))-{F}_{X}(a)] \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $u$ is monotonically increasing, we have that $u^{-1}$ is monotonically increasing as well. To see this, it's helpful to remember that we reflect a function over the line $y=x$ to get its inverse.\n\nSince the slope of a monotonically increasing function is always zero or positive, we have $(u^{-1})'(y) \\geq 0,$ and consequently $(u^{-1})'(y) = \\left| (u^{-1})'(y) \\right|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))|({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163272 #edb/ref 1065 5449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163272 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "20e03906-77e2-525c-8b4e-ca6bd91782de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163272 #edb/ref 1067 "Proof of the Change-of-Variables Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555024 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555024 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555024 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0cff411